   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In February 2022, after the adoption of the &#x201c;Strategy for Skills Development in Armenia&#x201d;,  the Republican Union of Employers of Armenia (RUEA) organized an online discussion to present it to its members and key partners. In July 2022, RUEA organized a conference where it engaged with the  Ministry of Education, Science, Culture and Sports, and stakeholders in education sector to discuss principal aspects of the country&#x2019;s skills strategy. Following the event, RUEA actively engaged with the Ministry of Education to promote its policy through periodic meetings, participation in working groups and presenting their paper in key national dialogue platforms. In November 2022, the Ministry of Education published the draft of a new Law on Education which reflected several key ideas outlined in the RUEA policy document. The draft law is under discussion in government as of August 2023.

In March 2022, RUEA took further strides by adopting a set of policy documents on key aspects of Armenia&#x2019;s business environment. These include: &#x201c;Business taxation in Armenia&#x201d;, &#x201c;Digitalization in Armenia&#x201d;, &#x201c;FDI and exports in Armenia&#x201d;, &#x201c;Market system analysis of fruit and vegetables processing in Armenia&#x201d;, &#x201c;Handling economic growth in conditions of COVID and informality in Armenia&#x201d;. RUEA produced  each of these policy papers based on extensive research and data analysis. As such, these documents serve as &quot;advocacy capital&quot; for RUEA, enabling the organization to provide informed input in policy discussions and challenge the government&#x2019;s exclusive hold on knowledge and information. In February-March 2022, RUEA presented and deliberated these policy documents with experts, relevant ministries and policy makers, members and other stakeholders. The ILO supported RUEA conduct quantitative and qualitative research among Armenian employers on the key business challenges in the field of skills and education. Specifically, the ILO helped the RUEA to design and execute a survey on business needs for skills and education. In doing so, the ILO supported RUEA secretariat staff to develop the survey methodology and analyse the survey findings. Finished in June 2021, the survey covered 1000 companies . The qualitative research included three focus groups (June 2021) and one round table (October 2021) to validate the results. Subsequently the ILO assisted RUEA with technical expertise to develop a Skills Strategy policy document and to organize a conference on the Skills Strategy at which to present and promote it.   

Furthermore, the ILO supported RUEA in deploying the Market System Analysis Tool as a major input in developing the fruit and vegetables processing policy document. 

The ILO also assisted RUEA to conduct a survey of 500 enterprises and the organization of a focus group on the subject of COVID impact and informality (Sep 2021-Feb. 2022), as well as for the conduct of a survey of 200 companies and the organization of three focus groups on the topic of reasons for SME closure (Feb 2022). These two activities were the main contributions to the development of the COVID and informality policy document.  

Furthermore, the ILO supported RUEA with technical expertise for the focus group discussions and interviews with employers for the development of the policy papers on FDI and exports, Business Taxation, and Digitalization. 

In all of the above, the ILO assistance to RUEA covered the collection and analysis of primary and secondary data and their aggregation into coherent policy documents with relevant policy proposals, as well as the organization of online round tables at which the policy papers were presented and discussed by members and policy makers.</narrative>
   </description>
